Under NC Medicaid Policy 10A, outpatient therapy services must be provided **according to a treatment plan (plan of care)**, and that plan must include all of the following:

- the **start and end date** of the therapy treatment plan, with a duration of **no more than six months**;
- the **discipline-specific treatment diagnosis** and any related medical diagnoses;
- the beneficiary’s **rehabilitative or habilitative potential**;
- **defined goals** for each therapeutic discipline that are **specific, measurable, and reasonably expected to be achieved** within the duration of the plan;
- the **skilled interventions, methodology, procedures, and specific programs** to be used;
- the **frequency of services**;
- the **length of each treatment visit in minutes**; and
- the **name, credentials, and signature** of the professional completing the treatment plan, **dated on or before the start date** of the treatment plan. [Clinical Coverage Policy 10A](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/10a-outpatient-specialized-therapies/download?attachment)

Two related requirements in Policy 10A are also important:

- **All services must be provided according to a treatment plan that meets Subsection 3.2.1.6**; and
- providers must **review and renew or revise treatment plans and goals at least every six calendar months**. [Clinical Coverage Policy 10A](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/10a-outpatient-specialized-therapies/download?attachment)

This is consistent with the federal outpatient rehabilitation rule, which requires a **written plan of treatment established before treatment begins** and says the plan must state the **type, amount, frequency, and duration** of therapy services, plus the **diagnosis and anticipated goals**. [42 CFR Part 410](https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-42/part-410)

## Bottom line
For NC Medicaid outpatient specialized therapies under **Policy 10A**, the plan of care must be a **written, discipline-specific therapy treatment plan**, completed **on or before the start date**, limited to **no more than six months**, and detailed enough to show the diagnosis, goals, interventions, frequency, visit length, prognosis/potential, and the signing professional’s credentials and signature. Policy 10A is current with an effective date of **February 1, 2025**.

Yes. A CAP/DA beneficiary can transfer, and the governing CAP/DA rule is the policy’s **“Transfers of Eligible Beneficiaries”** section. NC Medicaid requires the case management entities to coordinate the transfer so there is **no gap in services**. [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment), § 3.3 (effective Sept. 1, 2025; current based on amendment check).

## Rules governing transfers of eligible beneficiaries

Under **CAP/DA § 3.3**, before a transfer occurs, the CME must coordinate the transfer and complete these steps:

- determine the anticipated service start date;
- coordinate a transition plan between provider agencies;
- discuss and plan for the beneficiary’s health, safety, and well-being;
- initiate transfer of the electronic health record in the CAP Business System (e-CAP/DA) to the receiving county;
- arrange a home visit by the receiving entity within **5 business days** after the beneficiary moves to the new primary residence, to assess the home environment and identify health/welfare concerns; and
- coordinate services to begin on the **first date of transfer**. [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment), § 3.3.

## If the beneficiary is transferring to a different county

Section 3.3 gives more specific rules for county-to-county transfers:

- the transferring CME and receiving CME must coordinate the transfer on the agreed transfer date, or **within less than 30 calendar days of the request to transfer**;
- both agencies must discuss and plan for the beneficiary’s health, safety, and well-being;
- the electronic health record must be transferred to the receiving county;
- the receiving CME must arrange the home visit within **5 business days** of the move; and
- services must begin on the **first date of transfer into the receiving county**. [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment), § 3.3.

## Beneficiary choice of providers

CAP/DA also requires that the person-centered service plan protect the beneficiary’s **right to choose among providers** and the right to choose among **Medicaid-enrolled providers**, documented by a signed Freedom of Choice form. That supports the idea that beneficiaries are not locked into one provider, though the actual transfer process is governed by § 3.3. [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment), § 5.4.2 (effective Sept. 1, 2025; current based on amendment check).

## About the federal citation

I could verify that **42 CFR Part 413 § 413.64** concerns **payment rules to providers** on a reasonable-cost basis, but I could **not verify from the retrieved text that it governs CAP/DA beneficiary transfers between service providers**. So the direct rule for your question is the NC CAP/DA policy, especially **§ 3.3**, with **§ 5.4.2** supporting beneficiary provider choice. [42 CFR Part 413](https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-42/part-413), § 413.64 (current based on amendment check).

## Bottom line

A CAP/DA beneficiary **may transfer**, including to providers coordinated through another county, but the transfer must be managed by the CMEs under **CAP/DA § 3.3** to protect continuity of care, health and safety, timely record transfer, a post-move home visit, and service start on the transfer date.

If you want, I can also pull out the exact language that applies specifically to **switching provider agencies without moving counties**, as opposed to a county-to-county transfer.

Home modifications under CAP/DA must be tied to an identified need in the beneficiary’s plan of care and developed collaboratively with the multidisciplinary team. The policy says the beneficiary agrees to “work collaboratively with my multidisciplinary team to identify my needs for equipment, modification, technology, training and education and goods and services.” Home modifications are covered when they are aimed at improving or maximizing the beneficiary’s mobility, safety, independence, community integration, environmental/community accessibility, or addressing 24/7 coverage concerns. [[Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment), effective 2025-09-01, current; beneficiary agreement section; § 3.4.5]

## Home modification requirements under CAP/DA

CAP/DA covers home modifications in a primary private residence where the beneficiary lives if the residence is:
- owned by the beneficiary or family;
- rented, if the modifications are portable; or
- rented, if the owner allows the modifications. [[Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment), effective 2025-09-01, current]

Examples of covered home modifications include:
- ramps, grab bars, safety rails, and handrails;
- widening doorways or passages for wheelchair/walker access;
- emergency egress when medically necessary due to physical limitations of the responsible party;
- bathroom accessibility modifications;
- bedroom modifications for hospital beds and wheelchairs;
- kitchen accessibility modifications;
- floor coverings for ease of ambulation;
- non-skid surfaces;
- door handle and door modifications;
- hydraulic/manual/electronic lifts used primarily inside the home;
- smart home devices in certain circumstances;
- air filtration systems in certain medically justified circumstances; and
- replacement of fixtures, mirrors, and storage space removed or damaged during an approved modification. [[Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment), effective 2025-09-01, current]

The policy also includes related covered costs such as:
- technical assistance in device selection;
- training in device use by a qualified assistive technology professional;
- purchase costs, permits, inspections, taxes, and delivery charges;
- installation;
- assessment of the modification by the case manager and any applicable inspectors for safety and fit to beneficiary need; and
- repair, if the repair costs less than replacement and is not covered by warranty. [[Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment), effective 2025-09-01, current]

### Important limits
The policy excludes items that:
- are not of direct medical or remedial benefit;
- are covered elsewhere under the Medicaid State Plan or home health/DME benefits;
- are recreational;
- are of general utility to a non-disabled person;
- involve warranties or unrelated service agreements; or
- are otherwise specifically excluded, such as certain security systems and pool/spa equipment. [[Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment), effective 2025-09-01, current]

## Documentation the Multidisciplinary Team must provide

For equipment, technology, and **home modifications**, the policy requires documentation including:

- **An MDT recommendation** identifying the beneficiary’s need for the home modification requested;
- the recommendation must **state the cost** of the item or modification;
- a **statement of need** identified in the service plan;
- **evidence of medical need** recommended by the MDT or by a physician, when applicable;
- **shipping costs itemized** in the proposal request;
- any **other information required** for the specific item requested;
- when quotes are required for a purchase, adaptation, or modification, **NC Medicaid determines how many quotes are required**; and
- in special circumstances, **NC Medicaid or its designee determines which professional(s)** must make written recommendations. [[Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment), § 5.9.1, effective 2025-09-01, current]

Additional required documentation reflected in the policy includes:
- a **comprehensive Multidisciplinary declaration of need assessment** completed by the case manager identifying equipment, supply, adaptation, or modification needs;
- a **copy of the physician’s order**, when applicable;
- a **recommendation by an appropriate professional** identifying the beneficiary’s need for the requested adaptation or modification;
- the **estimated life of the equipment/modification** and the expected length of time the beneficiary will benefit from it; and
- an **invoice from the supplier** showing the date provided, cost, and related charges, maintained in the CAP Business system. [[Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment), effective 2025-09-01, current]

## Training documentation

If the request involves equipment related to the modification, the policy also requires:
- a **plan for training** the beneficiary and family on its use; and
- that the **case manager document completion of the training**, signed by the CAP/DA beneficiary or responsible party. [[Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment), § 5.9.1, effective 2025-09-01, current]

