Under NC Medicaid, an HIV case management provider must meet specific certification standards and can be decertified for a broad set of compliance, staffing, billing, documentation, and program-integrity failures.

## Certification requirements

NC Medicaid policy requires an HIV case management provider to:

- have a **documented successful record of at least 3 years** providing or managing HIV case management programs;
- ensure HIV case management services are provided by **qualified case managers** meeting the policy’s staff qualification standards;
- ensure HIV case managers are supervised by **qualified supervisors** meeting the policy’s supervisor standards;
- **enroll each physical site** with NC Medicaid as a provider of HIV case management services;
- meet all applicable **state and federal Medicaid participation laws**;
- **maintain certification** as a qualified HIV case management provider;
- demonstrate compliance with **initial and ongoing certification processes**;
- demonstrate compliance with **monitoring and evaluation of case management records through a quality improvement plan**;
- allow NC Medicaid or its designated contractor to **review beneficiary records and inspect agency operations and financial records**;
- notify NC Medicaid or its designated contractor of proposed changes—such as ownership, name, address, telephone number, or business dissolution—**within 30 calendar days of the proposed change and no later than 5 business days after the actual change**; and
- **achieve national accreditation within 1 year of Medicaid enrollment** from one of the designated accrediting agencies listed in the policy.  
  [Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) Case Management](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/12b-human-immunodeficiency-virus-hiv-case-management/download?attachment), § 6.2.1 (effective 2020-01-15; current per amendment check).

For recertification specifically, the provider must:

- submit a **complete and signed Recertification Application** within **60 calendar days of receipt**;
- submit copies of all items from the initial certification materials that have **changed** since initial certification;
- submit copies of all **HIV case manager and supervisor credentials**;
- submit **annual summaries of quality improvement activities**, including outcome metrics; and
- submit to a **recertification on-site visit**, including review of beneficiary records and other clinical or business documentation as needed.  
  [Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) Case Management](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/12b-human-immunodeficiency-virus-hiv-case-management/download?attachment), § 6.2.4 (effective 2020-01-15; current per amendment check).

The recertification checklist reinforces those requirements by asking whether the submission includes, among other things:

- all case managers and supervisors with **dates of employment**;
- all counties where HIV case management services are provided;
- services offered by the agency;
- operating hours and an **emergency after-hours plan**;
- accrediting agency information and dates, or an explanation;
- the frequency of **satisfaction surveys** and how results are used;
- numbers of Medicaid and Ryan White clients;
- attached policies on confidentiality, grievances, beneficiary rights, non-discrimination, code of ethics, conflict of interest, electronic records, Medicaid records/retention, freedom of choice, transfer/discharge, and abuse/neglect/exploitation; and
- attached documents including a **quality assurance policy**, quality assurance chart, organizational chart, community resources, ownership information, staff credentials, CCNC/PCP networking materials, and HR policies/procedures/plans.  
  [dma-3157 HIV Case Management Provider Recertification Application Checklist](https://policies.ncdhhs.gov/wp-content/uploads/dma-3157.pdf) (current per amendment check).

## When a provider can be decertified

NC Medicaid may decertify an HIV case management provider for any of the following:

- failure to provide **core service components**;
- **fraudulent billing practices**;
- owner conviction of a **felony**;
- failure to develop, submit, and implement a **written plan of correction** for unmet program requirements within **30 calendar days**;
- **falsification of records**;
- violation of a beneficiary’s **confidentiality**;
- employing staff who do not meet the policy’s **qualification criteria**;
- staff failure to complete required **mandatory basic training within 90 days of employment**;
- staff failure to obtain required **continuing education units**;
- failure to provide case management staff with supervision sufficient to meet beneficiaries’ needs;
- failure to submit required documentation within the time frames required by the policy, NC Medicaid, or its designated contractor;
- failure to provide documentation sufficient to support billing as required by the policy;
- failure to implement and enforce a **quality improvement program**;
- failure to timely notify NC Medicaid or its contractor of agency changes, causing inability to contact the provider;
- failure to comply with applicable **federal and state laws, regulations, state reimbursement plan, and Medicaid policies**;
- failure to enroll any beneficiaries within **4 months of certification**; or
- failure to achieve or maintain the policy’s **certification requirements**.  
  [Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) Case Management](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/12b-human-immunodeficiency-virus-hiv-case-management/download?attachment), § 6.2.5 (effective 2020-01-15; current per amendment check).

## Bottom line

A provider must be properly qualified, staffed, supervised, enrolled, accredited, quality-monitored, and responsive to Medicaid oversight to be certified and remain certified. Decertification can result not only from fraud or criminal issues, but also from noncompliance with staffing, training, documentation, confidentiality, correction-plan, notice, and quality-improvement requirements.

